WEBVTT

00:00:00.144 --> 00:00:03.087
Most of us met civics once,
in a room like this one.

00:00:03.507 --> 00:00:04.627
Three branches of government.

00:00:04.987 --> 00:00:07.827
How a bill becomes a
law. Checks and balances.

00:00:08.656 --> 00:00:10.985
You learned it for a
test, you passed the test,

00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:13.045
and then you never had
to use any of it again.

00:00:13.645 --> 00:00:14.745
Not because you didn't care.

00:00:14.785 --> 00:00:16.963
But because nothing in
your week ever asked you.

00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:19.920
Then one day something does ask.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.740
A ballot, or a meeting, or an argument
you did not want to be a part of.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.060
And the answer is in there
somewhere, under years of dust.

00:00:28.664 --> 00:00:32.602
So here is a different offer.
Not a test. A game show.

00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:35.853
This is the Civic Trivia Championships.

00:00:37.076 --> 00:00:39.866
Matches are quick. A
game is five questions.

00:00:40.226 --> 00:00:43.546
Each one gives you four
answers in twenty-five seconds.

00:00:44.179 --> 00:00:45.817
A right answer is a hundred points.

00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:48.777
Answer inside the first ten
seconds and it's fifty more.

00:00:49.397 --> 00:00:51.357
Inside twenty, twenty-five more.

00:00:52.037 --> 00:00:54.617
Knowing an answer is worth
more than working it out.

00:00:55.095 --> 00:00:58.595
Every question ends the same
way, whether you got it or not.

00:00:59.175 --> 00:01:03.215
The right answer, and an explanation
of why it's the right answer.

00:01:03.921 --> 00:01:05.202
Under that is where it came from.

00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:07.882
A named source, with a link you can open,

00:01:08.282 --> 00:01:09.993
so you never have to take our word for it.

00:01:10.505 --> 00:01:13.407
And if you think a question
is wrong, then flag it for us;

00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:16.367
it goes into a queue that a
real person works through.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.033
The questions are not one big pile.

00:01:19.613 --> 00:01:22.913
They come in collections,
usually by location or topic.

00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:26.165
There are federal ones,
state ones and city ones.

00:01:26.525 --> 00:01:28.405
The federal collection
is a good place to start,

00:01:28.405 --> 00:01:30.305
because it applies to each of us.

00:01:30.889 --> 00:01:33.438
A city collection asks about that city.

00:01:33.858 --> 00:01:37.738
Its council, its budget, its
measures, its own particular history.

00:01:38.438 --> 00:01:40.878
No civics textbook was
ever going to carry those,

00:01:40.878 --> 00:01:43.978
and that's the corner of
government standing closest to you.

00:01:44.594 --> 00:01:47.108
When you get a question right,
these little folks start

00:01:47.108 --> 00:01:48.328
showing up to cheer you on.

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.955
Between questions, they stay and hang out.

00:01:50.955 --> 00:01:54.235
They come back for another round
and they're still standing there.

00:01:54.775 --> 00:01:56.998
Then the next right
answer brings another one.

00:01:57.592 --> 00:01:59.838
Once there are enough of
them, they stop milling about

00:01:59.838 --> 00:02:01.078
and start building something.

00:02:01.645 --> 00:02:03.684
And if you get an answer wrong
that you had right before,

00:02:03.904 --> 00:02:06.904
then that one leaves. Your
crowd is what you know

00:02:06.904 --> 00:02:08.604
today, not what you once knew.

00:02:09.238 --> 00:02:12.083
You can play Civic Trivia
Championships without an account.

00:02:12.763 --> 00:02:14.763
Signed out, your crowd
lives in your browser.

00:02:15.243 --> 00:02:18.043
But when you sign in, it
remembers your progress,

00:02:18.043 --> 00:02:21.023
with your level and your place
on the week's leaderboard.

00:02:21.728 --> 00:02:25.635
It's fair to ask why a trivia game
sits inside a voting platform.

00:02:26.316 --> 00:02:30.336
A people who mean to be their own
governors must arm themselves with the

00:02:30.336 --> 00:02:31.916
power which knowledge gives.

00:02:33.476 --> 00:02:37.596
James Madison wrote that,
in a letter, in 1822.

00:02:37.596 --> 00:02:41.016
An informed citizen is not a
nice addition to a democracy.

00:02:41.016 --> 00:02:43.227
It is the condition of one.

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:45.055
And the collections are evolving.

00:02:45.535 --> 00:02:46.855
Some answers have end dates.

00:02:47.055 --> 00:02:49.515
'Who is your mayor?' is
only true until it is not,

00:02:49.515 --> 00:02:52.355
so that question expires
at the end of their term,

00:02:52.355 --> 00:02:55.183
and a replacement with the
right answer takes its place.

00:02:55.776 --> 00:03:00.362
It's live today, at ctc
dot empowered dot vote.

00:03:00.927 --> 00:03:03.060
Pick a collection. Five questions,

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:07.119
twenty-five seconds each.
Then see who turns up.

00:03:07.765 --> 00:03:09.684
That is the Civic Trivia Championships.

00:03:10.704 --> 00:03:12.064
Go find out what you know.
